ogle.com/file/d/1EvITR0ngnKQrAhIwsVYuZPr7qXBlZ27r/view\">AC026201.mp4</a>"], ["Phone photos", "Stockton Prison Workshop Sep 2026, 6 files, the names end in _c.jpeg. They are small. Keep them small on screen."], ["Spare bites", "01:47.9 to 01:50.5: “I had people that loved me and protected me.” · 04:18.3 to 04:22.8: “I would never even wish something like this to my worst enemy,”"], ["Transcript", "<a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1SyvsPiHThEaGqWFDuYj4Hd7tzeyylI6bqBtzj2A9mPg/edit\">FTC reels 2026 - machine transcripts</a>, part 1 · a machine transcript, caption what you hear"], ["visual.json", "No."], ["Music", "Low and simple."]], "tech": [["Source", "DSC_6589-003.mp4 (4:28): 3840×2160, 29.97 fps, one camera, 2 audio channels, sound from the camera. B-roll: 1920×1080, 23.98 fps, flat color."], ["Timeline", "1080×1920 at <b>29.97 fps</b>, the frame rate of the interview. The B-roll is 23.98: conform it."], ["Crop", "The interview is horizontal 4K. A 9:16 crop is 1215×2160, a little more than the timeline, so a small push in is safe. The B-roll is 1080p: a 9:16 crop is an upscale of about 178%."], ["Audio", "We did not record this. Interview sound as described in Source. B-roll sound is room noise: keep it very low or mute it."], ["Color", "The B-roll is flat. Add color correction to it."], ["Loudness", "-14 LUFS integrated, -1 dBTP peak. Music low and simple. The client said the sound effects in last year's videos were too much."], ["Captions", "Burned in for the whole video, low in the frame (client rule). Purple box, white text, key words in orange. Same style as the centerpiece."], ["Export", "H.264 MP4, 1080×1920, 9:16. Upload to Frame.io → Ready for Review."]], "checklist": ["Frame 1 is his face, already talking, with the hook text. No logo, name or fade.", "The hook text is on screen at 0:00 and does not animate on.", "Nothing from 01:26.6 to 01:42.6 is in the cut.", "The words “life without parole” are not said or shown. If they are, the small LWOP graphic is added.", "No former staff and no unknown face from an old clip.", "Captions sit low in the frame, purple box, white text, orange key words.", "The text says “The Freedom to Choose Project” or “FTC”. Never “FTCP”.", "The end card has the logo, “Change from the Inside” and space for the donation link.", "The file is 9:16 only."], "decisions": [{"t": "Donation link", "b": "The client has not sent the donation link. Leave space for one line on the end card."}, {"t": "Name spelling", "b": "“Gerardo Santos” is spelled by ear from the file. The client confirms it before captions lock."}], "client_note": "<b>Why this matters:</b> the client wants a confident, warm tone. The viewer is invited to join. The viewer is not asked to feel sorry.", "ask_examples": ["Which Gerardo lines belong to the centerpiece?", "What is the LWOP rule?", "What goes on the end card?", "Which B-roll can I use?"], "transcript": "Machine transcript of DSC_6589-003.mp4. Times are mm:ss in the file. Some words are wrong. Caption what you hear.\n[00:03] So my name is Gerardo Santos.\n[00:07] Perfect.\n[00:10] Can you tell us about your first time volunteering with Freedom to Choose Workshop?\n[00:15] So my first time, it was, I was nervous, right?\n[00:23] Not so much about going in to the prisons or being inside with any of the volunteers or the participants, but\n[00:37] rather, I think, just the facility itself, you know, having to go through that process.\n[00:43] But I was also excited because growing up, I had always gone through the visitor process, which is, you know, you get scammed.\n[00:55] It almost feels like your whole entire character or like person is being judged here by a sheriff.\n[01:07] Sometimes it was the saddest thing to see, you know, like there was just mothers, you know, sisters\n[01:19] and my brother.\n[01:20] And it's always sad to get turned away, especially after driving so far or whatever it is.\n[01:26] So I was excited this time because I was going to go in on different terms.\n[01:32] And I felt so much support.\n[01:35] I'll never forget that feeling.\n[01:38] I felt like everybody was there holding me.\n[01:45] Like I had a backup.\n[01:47] You know, I had people that loved me and protected me.\n[01:50] I didn't feel like a victim anymore walking in that processing room.\n[01:56] And it was empowering, right?\n[02:02] And so to me, just having growing up, like going to these prisons, I think that was the most, like, personally impactful position\n[02:14] that I could have ever, like, been placed in.\n[02:20] Yeah, I think that's what I have for that.\n[02:24] Of course, there's so much that I could say, too, about the actual experience, like with the trios.\n[02:31] I don't know if there's enough time for that, but...\n[02:33] Yeah, absolutely.\n[02:35] There is.\n[02:36] Thank you so much for sharing that.\n[02:38] Do you want to talk about something that, like, you remember that came up for you during one of the trios?\n[02:45] Yeah, almost immediately.\n[02:50] This gentleman told me, he sat down with me, you know.\n[02:54] We hadn't even sat down with the trio.\n[02:57] And I told him a little bit about my life, my history, who I am.\n[03:04] I told him my brother was incarcerated in 1994.\n[03:09] He got life without parole.\n[03:11] And he actually got me on to freedom to choose, which eventually led me to the actual workshop.\n[03:21] And he tells me something I'd never really realized, something I didn't know I needed to hear.\n[03:32] And, you know, because it wasn't the trio yet, I really tried fighting my tears back.\n[03:39] Because, I mean, it was already packed in, you know, and it wanted to come out.\n[03:43] But from what he told me, it just felt like it was meant to be what he had to tell me.\n[03:53] Well, I'll share, you know, what he said.\n[03:56] And that was that, he said it's harder for families to experience their loved ones in prison.\n[04:09] It's almost like experiencing somebody in their hospital bed, you know, if they're ill.\n[04:16] Even I said I would never even wish something like this to my worst enemy, for them to experience what a family member experiences."}